A result-generic template with one result that mentions every parameter
(Pair[T, U]) and another that mentions only a subset (Key[T]) is dispatched by
the demand that actually arrives, not by the all-covering result. Only Key[int]
is consumed, which pins T but leaves U free, so the template is instantiated at
F[int, U] with U lifted to the injector. (Without demand-aware dispatch this
would fall through both the worklist and the joint path and be wrongly rejected
as an unused template.)
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Key[T any] struct{}
type Pair[T, U any] struct{}

//plumb:build
func F[T, U any]() (Pair[T, U], Key[T]) { return Pair[T, U]{}, Key[T]{} }

//plumb:build
func Use(k Key[int]) int { return 0 }
